@@ -241,6 +243,8 @@ The code needs Python 3.15 or later. The image is built on `python:3.15-slim-tri
 
 **Coverage.** The tests are held to how settled the code is, not to one number for everything; git knows how long each line has stood (`git blame`), so nothing is marked by hand. *Fresh* code — not committed, or changed in the last 14 days — needs a test to reach each function (or server route, an `if path == "/…"` branch) it's in, and nothing more: one test at its seam while it's still moving. *Settled* code — unchanged for 14 days or more — needs 98% of its lines run by the tests; under that, the lines no test runs are listed by file. At release (`tnsync --release`, or `python3 dev/coverage.py --release`), 98% of every line and each file at 90% or more, red when it isn't met. What counts as code: every line that holds some, but docstrings, `def` and `class` lines (they run on import) and the `if __name__ == "__main__":` tail; nothing else is left out. `python3 tests/run.py --coverage` gathers it from every worker as the suite runs (`sys.monitoring`, every thread, each line watched until it first runs), into `~/.cache/plex-cache/coverage.json`, for that code only. The optimizer's tests that need MKVToolNix and ffmpeg run only where they're installed (inside the image on GitHub), so without them the optimizer's lines count as not run.
 
+**Translations.** The catalogs (`plexcache/web/i18n/<code>.json`) map each English text to its translation; the app shows English for anything still `null`, so a half-done language is safe to run. Between releases they may lag the code — the Translations block only tells how far they are, one number across every language. On `master`, and on a `--release` run, main must ship complete: `dev/i18n.py --tnsync` (the one place that decides, from `TNSYNC_MODE` and `TNSYNC_RELEASE`) then fails on anything untranslated. For a release: `python3 dev/i18n.py sync` brings every catalog in step with the code, translate what's `null` (`missing`/`merge` help), and `python3 dev/i18n.py status` says how far each language is.
+
 **Known vulnerabilities.** The same look (`dev/upkeep.py`, tnsync's Dependencies box) asks for the CVEs in what the app runs and is built with, once a day: OSV for the npm packages of `dev/package-lock.json` (and what pulls each in) and for oxipng, NVD for the ffmpeg, libjpeg-turbo and MKVToolNix the image builds, and Trivy (`dev/tools.toml`) for the image of the copy being checked, built here from it as `plex-cache:cves` (tnsync's copy: `tnsync main` main's, `tnsync dev` dev's, `tnsync local` your folder on its branch; the pins and npm lock are that copy's too). With Docker not running or the build failing, the image that copy's branch publishes instead, `dev` on dev and `latest` on main, said in a note (both when it isn't a checkout; their Debian packages, and the Python packages the base image carries; a private image needs `docker login ghcr.io`). A CVE is never a failure: one that needs doing turns the box yellow, with what to do. Only what needs doing is said: a fix a minor or patch release brings is moved as usual, its line saying what it fixes; a Debian or base-image fix is left to the weekly rebuild, and said only when the image still lacks it 8 days after it's first seen; a fix only in a major release, or in a version held by choice, is yours; a fix in a package another pulls in, that still asks for the old one, is said after 90 days (an npm override, a newer parent, or replacing it); no fix at all is upstream's while they're at it, and said once there's been none for 90 days, or no release for a year: replace it, patch it ourselves, or move to a maintained fork. A fix NVD gives only as a date in upstream's git (a snapshot range, no release named) is checked against when the release we have was tagged: made after it, it has the fix and isn't said. An image on another OS than the Dockerfile's (`latest`, built from an older one) is told its next build fixes it, not `apt-get`; and once the Dockerfile takes pip out, its packages' CVEs say the next build drops them. Both are listed apart, as fixed in dev and waiting on the release: notices, not counted, the box not orange for them. The day's answers are kept per version of `dev/cves.py`'s code (not its `DECISIONS`), so a change to it asks again the same day. A Debian CVE with no fix is Debian's call: counted, not listed. Once one's been decided, `DECISIONS` in `dev/cves.py` (the maintainer's only) records the choice, the date and why; it's looked at again after six months. On GitHub the image job checks the image it just built (`python dev/upkeep.py --cves plex-cache:test`): each one a warning on the run, never a failed build.
 
 **The future track.** `python3 dev/future.py` builds the image on the next Python's newest prerelease (`python:3.16.0a1-slim-trixie`, say, while the image runs 3.15) and runs the whole suite inside it with every warning an error. The tools come the same way: for each, upstream's newest pre-release when it's ahead of the stable release, else that stable release — Python's alphas, betas and rcs as Docker Hub tags them; Node's nightlies and rcs from nodejs.org (the page tests need node; the image serves no pages through it, so the build is fetched once, kept in `~/.cache/plex-cache/node/`, and mounted into the container); ffmpeg's git snapshot, built from source once a week (`FFMPEG_SOURCE` and `FFMPEG_STAMP` in the Dockerfile); libjpeg-turbo's and oxipng's GitHub pre-releases; every package `dev/package.json` pins (both sections) at the newest npm dist-tag ahead of `latest` (`next`, `beta`, `rc`…), else `latest`, installed in a copy of `dev/package.json` kept in `~/.cache/plex-cache/web-future/` and mounted over the checkout's `node_modules` (yours stay at the pins; a pre-release outside another package's peer range is installed with `--legacy-peer-deps`); MKVToolNix has releases only. The web's checks run a year ahead too: the software tried is about a year ahead of the image's, so `dev/web.js` takes `"futureBrowserslist"` (`last 4 years`) in place of `"browserslist"` (`PLEXCACHE_WEB_TARGETS=future`). The run starts with a table of what it chose, kept for a day in `~/.cache/plex-cache/future.json` (`--fresh` asks again; `python3 dev/upkeep.py` reports it too); upstream not reached means the pin. `--stable` keeps every tool at the Dockerfile's pin, with Debian's node; `--tag 3.15-slim-forky` will try the next Debian the same way. The build shows as one line that moves (its step, what it's doing, the time); its full output goes to a log, shown when the build fails, or on the terminal with `-v`. The suite then runs through `tests/run.py`, with its progress bar. It needs Docker with a running engine, and uses BuildKit when Docker has it (`docker buildx`; on a Mac, `brew install docker-buildx`, then the `cliPluginsExtraDirs` line brew prints in `~/.docker/config.json`), else Docker's legacy builder. `--local` uses a `python3.16` of your own (or `uv`, which fetches prerelease builds). It is local on purpose: GitHub shows how the app runs; this shows where it is going. When the next Python is released and the suite is clean on it, moving the image is `PYTHON_TAG` in the Dockerfile and `python-version` in `build.yml`.
